          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A claim came back asking for more specificity on a three-character code. Is that always a reasonable request?",
          "answer": "A three-character code is often only the broad category. The corpus says that \"the three-character form is the category,\" and that characters four and five add detail such as cause, anatomical site, or severity (source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*). It also says most ICD-10-CM categories need a fourth, fifth, or sixth character before the code is billable (source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*). So a request for more specificity can be correct when the code you were given is a category with subcodes.\n\nNot every three-character code works that way. I10 (essential hypertension) is complete at three characters, so a request to add specificity to it \"is a request the classification cannot satisfy\" (same source). Whether a request is reasonable therefore depends on the specific code.\n\nThe corpus does not cover how payers decide when to ask for more detail. It does not describe payer policies, claim edits, or what a particular payer requires. For the reason on your denial, ask the payer for the specific edit or policy cited, and ask your billing or coding staff to check whether the code you submitted has subcodes.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
